Negation, Classical Logic and Finding Derivations Chapter 1 could prove that things are so. It could not prove that something is not so. No rule of Chapter 1 shows that no rational number has square 2, or that x^2 + 1 is never 0; none lets a proof by cases say “this case cannot occur” and move on; and none explains why the sentence “if x^2 < 0, then x = 7” should count as true, when its hypothesis never holds and its conclusion has nothing to do with it. All three are questions about negation and contradiction, and ordinary proofs are full of them: “suppose not”, “which is absurd”, “we prove the contrapositive”. This chapter adds the rules for “not” and for contradiction one at a time, first the rules that use a negation, then the rule that proves one. It shows that a sequent, once proved, can be used as a rule of its own; gives “if and only if” its rules, and with them what a definition’s “if” means; and then meets the one rule that is different in kind, proof by contradiction, together with the reason why it, double-negation elimination, and the law of excluded middle stand or fall together. It ends with a method for finding derivations, which collects the moves the chapter has met one section at a time, and with a question the method cannot answer: we show that something is derivable by exhibiting a derivation, but how could we ever show that something is not derivable? Chapter 3 answers it (@cor-refuting-row). What you need. All of Chapter 1: the rules for “and”, “implies”, and “or”, and above all the definition of a derivation, with its boxes and its scope rule (@def-derivation). School algebra about integers and real numbers is used in examples, as facts. Time. About 30 hours on the core track (all of A, one B and one C per section, the exit checks); about 45 hours for everything. Roadmap - Contradiction: using a negation. The rules ulename{
eg E} and ulename{ ot E}: what a clash is, what it buys, why an impossible case closes itself, and why an implication with an impossible hypothesis is true. - Proving a negation. The rule ulename{
eg I}, assume and reach a contradiction, and with it the proof that \sqrt 2 is irrational. - Derived rules. A sequent, once proved, may be used as a single line; modus tollens, the contrapositive, and disjunctive syllogism become rules. - Iff, equivalence, and what a definition says. The rules for \leftrightarrow, provable equivalence, chains of equivalences, and why a definition’s “if” means “if and only if”. - Proof by contradiction and the classical rules. The rule ulename{RAA}, the classical laws it gives (double negation, excluded middle, De Morgan, Peirce), and why three of them are one principle. - How to find a derivation. The search method, assembled from the moves of the chapter, with worked searches, one search that fails, and all the rules in one place. Named moves - Goal  ot: find a clash. Look for \varphi and 
eg\varphi among the available lines, or make \varphi a subgoal for some 
eg\varphi you have. - An impossible case closes itself. In a proof by cases, a case that reaches  ot ends by ulename{ ot E} on the current goal. - To prove “not \varphi”, assume \varphi. The box opens with the unnegated statement and ends on  ot. - Use a derived rule as one line. A proved sequent is a new rule. - Two boxes for an iff. One box for each direction, each ending on the other side. - When stuck, assume the negation of the goal. Proof by contradiction, and then hunt for a clash. - Work from both ends. The full method: read the skeleton off the goal, use the premises by their main connectives, and keep the classical step for last.

Proving a Negation The rules of Section 2.1 use a negation: ulename{
eg E} records a clash, and ulename{ ot E} turns a clash into any statement we like. Neither rule can prove a negation that was not already there. So we still cannot prove that no rational number has square 2, or even that x^2 + 1 is never 0. This section adds the one rule that proves a negation, ulename{
eg I}, and uses it for one of the oldest proofs in mathematics. How to prove that something is not so To show that no rational number has square 2, we cannot inspect the rational numbers one by one: there are infinitely many. A mathematician instead supposes the opposite and shows that it leads to something impossible: “suppose r is rational and r^2 = 2; …; this is a contradiction; hence r^2 
e 2.” Try the smaller goal x^2 + 1 
e 0, for a real number x, with the rules so far. The goal is 
eg(x^2 + 1 = 0), and its main connective is 
eg. Chapter 1’s goal-directed move asks for the introduction rule of the main connective, and 
eg has none yet. The rules of Section 2.1 do not help: ulename{ ot E} could write the goal, but only from a line  ot, and nothing available clashes. The statement x^2 + 1 = 0 is not a line of anything; it is the thing we want to rule out. The mathematician’s sentence shows the missing rule. “Suppose r^2 = 2” opens a box, exactly like the box of ulename{{	o}I} in @def-rules-implication, and “this is a contradiction” is its last line,  ot. The only new thing is what the box gives when it closes. To prove “not \varphi”, assume \varphi and reach a contradiction. [Rules for Negation] The left schema is ulename{
eg I} (not-introduction): the assumption \varphi is discharged when the box closes, and the line  ot is a line of that box itself, not of a box inside it. The right schema is ulename{
eg E}, recalled from @def-rules-contradiction, so that the rule that proves a negation and the rule that uses one stand together. Read the schema of ulename{
eg I} clause by clause. 1. Open a box whose first line is the assumption \varphi. It is the unnegated statement: the thing we want to refute, not the thing we want to prove. Any statement may be assumed. 2. Inside the box every rule may be used, and every line available just above the box may be cited, as in any box (@def-derivation). 3. The last line of the box is exactly  ot, and it is a line of that box itself, not inside a further box opened within it. This is the same wording as for ulename{{	o}I}, and every box rule shares it; Chapter 3’s soundness proof (@thm-soundness) relies on it. 4. Close the box and write 
eg\varphi on the next line, outside it. The justification cites the box as a range, “ulename{
eg I} i–j”. The assumption is discharged: after the box closes, nothing inside it may be cited, and only the negation leaves. 5. The new line is always a negation. ulename{
eg I} never writes a statement whose main connective is not 
eg. The degenerate case is a box of one line, whose assumption is already  ot. The box \lines{1}{1} then ends on  ot as soon as it opens, and ulename{
eg I} gives 
eg ot: So \vdash 
eg ot: “it is not the case that contradiction” is derivable from nothing, as it should be. A noninstance by one change. Take a box opened by \varphi whose last line is 
eg\varphi instead of  ot. It looks as if the assumption has refuted itself, and ulename{
eg I} still does not apply: the box must end on  ot. One more line,  ot by ulename{
eg E} citing the assumption and the line 
eg\varphi, repairs it. The same holds if  ot is reached only inside a smaller box within the box: it is not then a line of the box itself. Why this rule. The statement 
eg\varphi says that \varphi fails, and the box shows what would happen if \varphi held: together with the lines above it, it would lead to  ot, which never holds. So in any situation where the lines above the box hold, \varphi cannot hold. That is exactly what 
eg\varphi claims. As for Section 2.1’s rules, Chapter 3 turns this into a proof (@thm-soundness). The rules for 
eg have the same shapes as rules we already know, with  ot in one slot. Put them beside the rules for 	o, applied to the statement \varphi 	o  ot: ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- To … a negation 
eg\varphi an implication \varphi 	o  ot ----------------------- -------------------------------------------------- -------------------------------------------------- prove it box from \varphi to  ot, then ulename{
eg I} box from \varphi to  ot, then ulename{{	o}I} use it, given \varphi  ot by ulename{
eg E}  ot by ulename{{	o}E} ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Row by row, 
eg\varphi behaves exactly like \varphi 	o  ot: “not \varphi” means “\varphi leads to contradiction”. Some presentations of logic take this as the definition of 
eg. This book keeps 
eg as a connective with its own rules, and Chapter 5 returns to the reading 
eg\varphi \approx \varphi 	o  ot when it asks what a proof of a negation provides. ulename{
eg I} proves negative statements only. Its box opens with \varphi and ends by writing 
eg\varphi. A proof that says “suppose x is not positive; …; contradiction; hence x is positive” has a box opened by 
eg(x > 0), and ulename{
eg I} applied to that box gives 
eg
eg(x > 0), not x > 0. Getting from there to x > 0 needs a rule we have not met. Section 2.5 is about that rule, and contrasts the two kinds of box in full. Do not open the box with what you want to prove. To prove 
eg\varphi, the box opens with \varphi. A box opened by 
eg\varphi and ending in  ot gives 
eg
eg\varphi by ulename{
eg I}, the negation of the goal, not the goal. This is the negation’s version of the converse error that Section 1.5 found in Argument B. First derivations with not-introduction The goal-directed move of this section: a goal 
eg\varphi opens a box with \varphi, and the new goal is  ot. From there, Section 2.1’s move takes over: goal  ot, find a clash, among the available lines or by manufacturing its missing half. [Two negations from one premise] Show that (a) P \vdash 
eg
eg P, and (b) (P \vee Q) 	o 
eg P \vdash 
eg P. (a) The goal 
eg
eg P is 
eg(
eg P), a negation of 
eg P. So the box opens with what is negated, 
eg P, and aims at  ot. The clash is immediate: the premise P and the assumption 
eg P. (b) The goal 
eg P opens a box on P. Inside it the premise is an implication, and the premise-directed move feeds it its hypothesis P \vee Q, which ulename{\vee I} manufactures from P. The premise then hands back 
eg P, and that clashes with the box’s own assumption. The assumption is used twice: once to build the hypothesis, once in the clash. (a) Four lines. (b) Six lines. In (b), line 4 already says 
eg P, but it lies inside the box of P and depends on that assumption, so it cannot be the conclusion. Line 6 is the same statement outside the box, where it depends on the premise alone. Derivation (a) adds two negations. The reverse passage, from 
eg
eg P alone to P, would have to remove two, and no rule so far has a negation as its one given line and the unnegated statement as its new line: ulename{
eg E} needs a second line and writes only  ot, and ulename{
eg I} only adds a 
eg. Section 2.5 returns to this. Is 
eg(P \vee Q) \vdash 
eg P? If so, give a derivation and name its rules. Compare it with @exm-manufactured-clash, which has the same premise. Yes. The goal 
eg P is a negation, so assume P and aim at  ot; ulename{\vee I} turns P into P \vee Q, which clashes with the premise. The rules are ulename{\vee I}, ulename{
eg E}, and ulename{
eg I}. The box is the same as in @exm-manufactured-clash up to its line  ot. There the goal was P 	o R, so the box had to end on R, written by ulename{ ot E}, and closed by ulename{{	o}I}. Here the goal is 
eg P, so the box ends on  ot itself and closes by ulename{
eg I}. In words: if neither P nor Q holds, then in particular P does not. In the next derivation the clash itself creates a goal that is a negation, and the move of this section is used a second time, one box inside another. [Three negations become one] Show that 
eg
eg
eg P \vdash 
eg P. The goal 
eg P opens a box on P, with goal  ot. Which clash? The only negation available is the premise 
eg(
eg
eg P), so the premise-directed question asks for its unnegated half, 
eg
eg P: that becomes the subgoal. It is itself a negation, 
eg(
eg P), so the goal-directed move opens a second ulename{
eg I} box, on 
eg P, with goal  ot. Inside it, 
eg P clashes with the outer assumption P. This is the first derivation in which a ulename{
eg I} box is opened for a subgoal that a clash created. Seven lines. Line 4 may cite line 2, because the only box containing line 2, the box \lines{2}{6}, also contains line 4 (@def-derivation). The inner box \lines{3}{4} ends on  ot as a line of its own, and so does the outer box \lines{2}{6}, at line 6; each ulename{
eg I} cites its own box. Notice what did not happen: no line removes a pair of negations. Three became one by adding them, twice, and clashing. A box may reach  ot without ever using its assumption. That is legal, but it is a sign: if no line of the box depends on \varphi, the same lines could have been written just above the box, where  ot would then stand, and ulename{ ot E} would have written 
eg\varphi, or anything else, directly. The lines available above the box were already contradictory. When your ulename{
eg I} box never uses its assumption, look for the error above the box, not in it. The first example about numbers is the goal we could not reach at the start of the section. [A negative statement proved by not-introduction] Let x be a real number. Prove that x^2 + 1 
e 0, naming the rules. The goal x^2 + 1 
e 0 is 
eg(x^2 + 1 = 0), a negation, so open a box on x^2 + 1 = 0 and look for a clash. The school fact x^2 \ge 0, that is 
eg(x^2 < 0), is the negation available; its unnegated half x^2 < 0 is the subgoal, and algebra on the assumption produces it. The goal x^2 + 1 
e 0 is 
eg(x^2 + 1 = 0), so we use ulename{
eg I}. Suppose x^2 + 1 = 0 [	ext{assumption for } ulename{
eg I}]. Then x^2 = -1, so x^2 < 0. But x^2 \ge 0, that is, 
eg(x^2 < 0), so we have a contradiction [ulename{
eg E}]. Hence x^2 + 1 
e 0 [ulename{
eg I}]. How the rule looks in a mathematical proof The proof that no rational number has square 2 has the shape of ulename{
eg I}: the claim is negative, so we assume its opposite and look for  ot. The tool that finds the clash is @thm-square-even-implies-even, and the school fact it needs is about writing fractions: (L) Every rational number can be written as p/q with p and q integers, q 
e 0, and p and q not both even. This holds because a fraction whose numerator and denominator are both even can be reduced by 2, and the reduction cannot go on for ever, since the denominator shrinks in absolute value each time. Making “cannot go on for ever” precise needs the well-ordering of the natural numbers, which comes in Sets, Relations, and Cardinality, Chapter 2; here we take (L) as known. [Irrationality of the Square Root of Two] There is no rational number whose square is 2. In other words, \sqrt{2} is irrational. Fix a rational number r and prove the negative statement r^2 
e 2 by ulename{
eg I}: assume r^2 = 2 and hunt for  ot. Write r = p/q as in (L), so that the equation becomes p^2 = 2q^2. The only available negation is “not both even”, so the clash must come from showing that p and q are both even; @thm-square-even-implies-even is the tool for that, used twice. Before reading on: the second use of @thm-square-even-implies-even needs the line “q^2 is even”. From which two earlier lines of the proof will it come? Let r be a rational number, and suppose r^2 = 2 [	ext{assumption for } ulename{
eg I}]. By (L), r = p/q for integers p and q with q 
e 0, not both even. Then p^2 = r^2 q^2 = 2q^2, so p^2 is even. By @thm-square-even-implies-even, p is even [ulename{{	o}E}], say p = 2m for an integer m. Then 4m^2 = 2q^2, so q^2 = 2m^2, and q^2 is even. By @thm-square-even-implies-even again, q is even [ulename{{	o}E}]. So p and q are both even [ulename{\wedge I}], while they were chosen not both even; this is a contradiction [ulename{
eg E}]. Hence r^2 
e 2 [ulename{
eg I}]. Since r was an arbitrary rational number, no rational number has square 2. This proves the theorem. In the proof, which step is ulename{
eg E}, and which two statements does it cite? The step “this is a contradiction”. It cites “p and q are both even”, derived inside the box of r^2 = 2 by ulename{\wedge I}, and “p and q are not both even”, which (L) supplied when p and q were chosen. In the derivation below these are lines 11 and 2, and the step is line 12. The propositional part of the proof fits in a derivation. Fix r and the integers p, q that (L) supplies. Lines 1 and 2 record what (L) says about them; lines 3 and 4 are @thm-square-even-implies-even for n = p and n = q; the lines marked “algebra” are the computations of the proof. Line 13 is r^2 
e 2. The box \lines{5}{12} is a ulename{
eg I} box as the schema asks: opened by the unnegated statement r^2 = 2, and ending on  ot as a line of its own. Two steps of the proof lie outside the derivation: choosing p, q, and m, and passing from the arbitrary r to every rational number. Both are quantifier rules, which come in Chapter 4. Notice also what the proof does not need: that \sqrt{2} exists as a real number. The theorem is a statement about rational numbers only. Analysis on the Real Line, Chapter 1 uses it in exactly this form, as evidence that the rational numbers have a gap where \sqrt{2} should be. And notice which rule closes it. “Suppose r^2 = 2” opens a box whose negation is the goal, so the proof is ulename{
eg I}, even though it is what everyone calls a proof by contradiction. Section 2.5 meets a second kind of box, which looks the same on the page and is a different rule; the distinction starts from the warning above about 
eg(x > 0). What the rule sounds like Here is the row of the rule, phrase, and Lean table for this section’s rule; the rows for ulename{
eg E} and ulename{ ot E} are in Section 2.1. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Rule You write Lean ------------------- ------------------------------------------------------------------ ------------------------- ulename{
eg I} “Suppose \varphi. … This is a contradiction. Hence not \varphi.” intro hp (goal False) ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- In Lean, the negation of P is defined to be P → False, with False playing the part of  ot: the reading of the remark above. So intro performs both ulename{{	o}I} and ulename{
eg I}. Exercises A. Check your understanding [A1] 1. Write the schema of ulename{
eg I} from memory, and say which statement opens its box. 2. Which rule does this sentence use, and which box does it close? “Suppose 0 had a reciprocal y. Then 1 = 0 \cdot y = 0, which is absurd. So 0 has no reciprocal.” 3. In the proof of @thm-sqrt2-irrational, the sentence “p is even” follows from “p^2 is even”. Which result and which rule justify it? 4. Where in the proof of @thm-sqrt2-irrational is (L) used, and what would go wrong if (L) said only “r = p/q with q 
e 0”? 5. Run the proof of @thm-sqrt2-irrational with 4 in place of 2: assume r^2 = 4 and write r = p/q as in (L). Which step can no longer be justified? (The conclusion “no rational number has square 4” is false, so some step must fail.) (a) The schema is the left one of @def-rules-negation: a box opened by the assumption \varphi, ending on  ot as a line of the box itself, then 
eg\varphi outside it, citing the box. The box opens with the unnegated statement \varphi. (b) ulename{
eg I}. The conclusion is the negative statement “0 has no reciprocal”; the box opens with “0 has a reciprocal y”, and “which is absurd” is its line  ot, reached by ulename{
eg E} from 1 = 0 and the fact 1 
e 0. (c) @thm-square-even-implies-even, for n = p, is the implication “if p^2 is even, then p is even”; together with “p^2 is even” it gives “p is even” by ulename{{	o}E} (line 8 of the derivation, citing lines 3 and 7). (d) The fact (L) supplies the line “p and q are not both even” (line 2 of the derivation), which the ulename{
eg E} of line 12 cites. Without that clause the proof still derives “both even”, but it contradicts nothing (2 = 4/2 has both even), and no  ot is reached. (e) With r^2 = 4 we get p^2 = 4q^2 = 2(2q^2), so p^2 is even and p is even, p = 2k. Then 4k^2 = 4q^2, so q^2 = k^2. This is where the proof stalls: nothing says q^2 is even, so @thm-square-even-implies-even cannot be applied to q. Indeed for r = 2 = 2/1 we have q = 1, which is odd. Every step before the stall is valid; the proof simply fails to reach  ot. B. Practice [B1: A box inside a box] Show that P 	o 
eg Q \vdash Q 	o 
eg P. Name the move that opens each box. The goal is an implication, so the outer box opens on Q, with goal 
eg P. That goal is a negation, so the inner box opens on P, with goal  ot. Inside it, the premise fires on P and gives 
eg Q, which clashes with the outer assumption Q. Line 5 may cite line 2 because the box \lines{2}{6} contains line 5. Both boxes were read off the goal: the outer one by Chapter 1’s move for an implication, the inner one by this section’s move for a negation. In words: if P rules out Q, then Q rules out P. [B2: A quadratic with no zero] Let x be a real number. Prove that x^2 - x + 1 
e 0, naming the rules. Hint: complete the square. The goal is 
eg(x^2 - x + 1 = 0), a negation, so we use the rule ulename{
eg I}. Suppose that x^2 - x + 1 = 0, as the assumption for ulename{
eg I}. Completing the square, x^2 - x + 1 = \left( x - 	frac12 ight)^2 + 	frac34 , so (x - 	frac12)^2 = -	frac34, and therefore (x - 	frac12)^2 < 0. But the square of a real number is never negative, that is, 
eg((x - 	frac12)^2 < 0); this is a contradiction [ulename{
eg E}]. Hence x^2 - x + 1 
e 0 [ulename{
eg I}]. This is the pattern of @exm-square-plus-one, with the square (x - 	frac12)^2 in place of x^2. The identity can be checked by expanding: (x - 	frac12)^2 = x^2 - x + 	frac14, and 	frac14 + 	frac34 = 1. [B3: The square root of three] You may use that every integer can be written as 3k, 3k + 1 or 3k + 2 with k an integer, in one way only, and that every rational number can be written as p/q with integers p, q, q 
e 0, and p, q not both divisible by 3. 1. Prove that if n is an integer and n^2 is divisible by 3, then n is divisible by 3. 2. Hence prove that no rational number has square 3. (a) Suppose n^2 is divisible by 3 [	ext{assumption for } ulename{{	o}I}], so n^2 has the form 3j with j an integer. The integer n has the form 3k, 3k + 1 or 3k + 2, and we argue by cases [ulename{\vee E}]; a three-way split is two uses of ulename{\vee E}, one inside a box of the other. Case n = 3k. Then n is divisible by 3, as required. Cases n = 3k + 1 and n = 3k + 2. Then  egin{aligned} (3k + 1)^2 &= 9k^2 + 6k + 1 = 3(3k^2 + 2k) + 1, \ (3k + 2)^2 &= 9k^2 + 12k + 4 = 3(3k^2 + 4k + 1) + 1 , \end{aligned} so n^2 has the form 3j' + 1 as well as the form 3j. An integer has only one of the three forms, so this is a contradiction [ulename{
eg E}], and the case cannot occur. By ulename{ ot E}, n is divisible by 3 in this case too. All cases give that n is divisible by 3 [ulename{\vee E}], which proves (a) [ulename{{	o}I}]. The two impossible cases were closed exactly as the odd case in the proof of @thm-square-even-implies-even. (b) Let r be a rational number and suppose r^2 = 3 [	ext{assumption for } ulename{
eg I}]. Write r = p/q with p, q not both divisible by 3. Then p^2 = 3q^2, so 3 divides p^2, and by (a), p = 3m for an integer m. Then 9m^2 = 3q^2, so q^2 = 3m^2, and by (a) again, 3 divides q. So 3 divides both p and q [ulename{\wedge I}]; this contradicts their choice [ulename{
eg E}]. Hence r^2 
e 3 [ulename{
eg I}]. Since r was an arbitrary rational number, no rational number has square 3. The form of (L) matters: the argument ends at “3 divides p and q”, which clashes only with “not both divisible by 3”. The form “not both even” of the text would give no clash, since p = q = 3 are both divisible by 3 and not both even. [B4: A premise that turns on itself] Show that P 	o Q, \ Q 	o 
eg P \vdash 
eg P. Then say in one sentence what the sequent means. The goal 
eg P is a negation, so assume P. The two premises, used in turn, turn P into 
eg P, and the clash gives  ot. In words: if P leads to Q, and Q rules P out, then P refutes itself, and so P fails. Line 5 is the moment of self-refutation: the assumption has produced its own negation. Line 6 is still needed, because the box must end on  ot, not on 
eg P. C. Going deeper [C1: The square root of six] Prove that no rational number has square 6. Use only the facts (E1)–(E3) of Section 2.1, (L), and @thm-square-even-implies-even, and name the rule at each logical step. [Hint 1] Follow the proof of @thm-sqrt2-irrational. After writing p = 2m, you will need to show that q is even without knowing that q^2 is even. [Hint 2] You will reach 3q^2 = 2m^2, so 3q^2 is even. The proof of @thm-square-even-implies-even passed from “n^2 is even” to “n is even”; imitate its shape for 3q^2 and q. Let r be a rational number and suppose r^2 = 6 [	ext{assumption for } ulename{
eg I}]. By (L), r = p/q with integers p, q, q 
e 0, not both even. Then p^2 = 6q^2 = 2(3q^2) is even, so p is even by @thm-square-even-implies-even [ulename{{	o}E}]; write p = 2m. Then 4m^2 = 6q^2, that is, 3q^2 = 2m^2, so 3q^2 is even. We show that q is even. By (E1), q is even or odd, and we argue by cases [ulename{\vee E}]. Case q even: this is the goal. Case q odd: then q^2 is odd by (E3) [ulename{{	o}E}], say q^2 = 2j + 1, and then 3q^2 = 6j + 3 = 2(3j + 1) + 1 is odd. So 3q^2 is both even and odd [ulename{\wedge I}]; this contradicts (E2) [ulename{
eg E}], so this case cannot occur, and in it q is even [ulename{ ot E}]. In both cases q is even [ulename{\vee E}]. So p and q are both even [ulename{\wedge I}]; this contradicts (L) [ulename{
eg E}]. Hence r^2 
e 6 [ulename{
eg I}], and since r was arbitrary, no rational number has square 6. Marking scheme. (10 points) - 2: opens with ulename{
eg I}: a fixed rational r, the assumption r^2 = 6, and r = p/q with p, q not both even from (L). - 2: derives p even from p^2 = 2(3q^2) by @thm-square-even-implies-even. - 3: derives q even from 3q^2 = 2m^2, with the odd case ruled out by a correct parity computation and (E2). - 2: reaches  ot by clashing “both even” with (L), and concludes r^2 
