The Connective Rules in Prose Chapters 1–4 built a calculus in which every step of a proof is one rule applied to lines that are available. Mathematicians do not write Fitch boxes, and from Part I on neither does this book; yet every proof in it is a derivation underneath, written in sentences. This section and the next compile the dictionary between the two: for each rule, the phrases that carry it in written mathematics, a sentence of analysis in which it does its work, and whether a writer may leave it unsaid. This section covers the connectives and the classical rules; Section 5.2 covers the quantifiers, equality and induction. The same proof, twice Section 2.1 proved @thm-square-even-implies-even (“if n^2 is even, then n is even”) by cases, and printed a table that matched each line of its derivation with one sentence of the proof. Section 2.3 proved the same theorem a second way, in @exm-square-even-via-contrapositive, with the derived rules ulename{CP} and ulename{DS} doing the work. Here is that second derivation again, with E: “n is even”, O: “n is odd”, S: “n^2 is even”, T: “n^2 is odd”, and the facts (E1)–(E3) of Section 2.1 (every integer is even or odd; no integer is both; the square of an odd integer is odd) as its premises. A mathematician would write the same proof in four sentences: Suppose n^2 is even. If n^2 were odd, it would be both even and odd, which (E2) forbids; so n^2 is not odd. By (E3), read contrapositively, n is not odd. Since n is even or odd, by (E1), n is even. The paragraph accounts for every line of the derivation, though some lines have no words of their own. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Lines Words that carry them Rule ----------------------- ----------------------------------------------- ------------------------------------- 4 “Suppose n^2 is even.” ulename{{	o}I}, the box opens 5 “If n^2 were odd” ulename{
