Four Levels of One Proof Sections 5.1 and 5.2 matched each rule with the phrases that carry it. A dictionary works one word at a time; a proof is read and written whole. This section puts the dictionary to work on two complete proofs, the irrationality of \sqrt 2 and “uniformly continuous implies continuous”, and writes each of them four times: as a full derivation, as a skeleton, as prose with every rule tagged, and as the plain prose of a textbook. The four texts are one proof written for four different readers. The section names the four levels, fixes exactly which steps the last level may leave unsaid, and ends with the use of the levels that matters most to a reader working alone: when a sentence looks doubtful, rewrite it, and only it, at level 1, as a skeleton, and look. One proof, four texts The book has already printed some proofs twice. In Section 2.2 the proof of @thm-sqrt2-irrational appears as a paragraph with a rule in brackets after several of its steps, and then, below it, as a 13-line derivation of its propositional part. In Section 4.10, @exm-uniform-implies-continuous is an 11-line derivation, followed by three sentences of prose with line numbers in brackets. In both places the derivation and the paragraph say the same thing, and the reader is asked to match them. What was not said is how many such texts there are, what each leaves out, and who each is for. There are four, and the differences between them are not matters of taste. A derivation, a skeleton and a paragraph are the same proof; they differ in what the reader is trusted to restore. [Levels of Proof] A proof can be written at four levels. - Level 0, the full derivation. A derivation in the sense of @def-derivation, with the rules of Chapters 1, 2 and 4: every line is a premise, an assumption, or one rule applied to available lines. A derived rule used as one line (@def-derived-rule), such as the bounded rules of @prp-bounded-quantifier-rules, counts as one rule. Facts about numbers enter as premises, or as lines justified “arith”, each of which records one such fact applied to the lines it cites. - Level 1, the skeleton. The boxes and the logical lines only: every assumption, every flagged name, every line that opens or closes a box, with the rule that closes it, and every line that such a closing line cites. Each stretch of mathematics between them is compressed into one line, justified “arith”, “by def”, or by a cited result; a stretch of routine logical steps inside one box is compressed into one line justified by the names of its rules in order, such as “ulename{ orall E}, ulename{\exists I}”. - Level 2, annotated prose. Sentences, each tagged in brackets with the rule or rules it carries, such as [ulename{ orall I}] or [ulename{\exists E}]. - Level 3, plain prose. Sentences without tags, in which some steps are left unsaid. Which steps may be left unsaid is fixed by the convention that follows, not by the writer. In words: level 0 hides no logic. Every line other than an arith line can be checked mechanically against the schema of its rule, and each arith line records one fact about numbers that can be checked by hand. Level 1 keeps the logical architecture and lets the arithmetic run in blocks. Level 2 is level 1 turned into sentences, with the architecture still labeled. Level 3 removes the labels and some of the lines, following a fixed convention. The levels are already on the page in earlier chapters. The derivation in Section 4.10 is level 0. The paragraph after @thm-sqrt2-irrational, with its tags [ulename{{	o}E}], [ulename{\wedge I}], [ulename{
eg E}], [ulename{
eg I}], is level 2 for the rules Chapter 2 had; its quantifier steps carry no tags. The derivation printed below that paragraph is not quite level 0, and the reason is instructive: it fixes r, p and q in advance and leaves out the two quantifier steps, choosing p, q (and later m) and passing from one r to every r, because Chapter 2 had no rules for them yet. Section 2.2 says so in the paragraph after the derivation. With Chapter 4’s rules those steps can be written. The example below writes the choices of p and q and the step to every r; the choice of m stays inside the arithmetic fact of its line 15, since evenness is treated there as a single predicate E. A non-example, by one change: take a level-1 skeleton and delete one of its boxes, say the box in which a name N_0 is chosen, keeping the lines inside. The result is not a shorter skeleton. It is a list in which N_0 appears from nowhere, and no reader can tell whether it was chosen, given, or assumed. A skeleton may compress the work inside a box; it may not compress the boxes. Why four levels and not two? Because each serves a task. Level 0 is for settling a doubt completely: nothing in it is taken on trust. Level 1 is for planning a proof and for reading one: it is what you write on scratch paper before the first sentence (Section 5.5 turns this into a procedure), and what you reconstruct from a textbook when a proof will not come clear (Section 5.4). Level 2 is for learning, which is why Part 0 (Logic and Proof, Chapters 1–5, and Sets, Relations, and Cardinality, Chapters 1–3) uses it and later Parts do not: from Analysis on the Real Line, Chapter 1, the start of Part I, proofs are written at level 3, in plain prose, and a rule tag appears only where the rule is itself the point, as in a review exercise that looks back at Part 0. Level 3 is what mathematicians read and write. A skeleton is not a finished proof. A level-1 text with lines such as “\abs{x_0 - y} < \delta_0 	o \abs{f(x_0) - f(y)} < \varepsilon_0, arith” is a plan: it says that a computation goes there, and it can hide a computation that does not work. For f(x) = x^2 on 
R, a skeleton of “f is uniformly continuous” with one line “arith” at that point looks exactly like a correct skeleton for f(x) = 3x + 1, where \delta = \varepsilon/3 works because \abs{(3x+1)-(3y+1)} = 3\abs{x-y}; the claim is false for the first function (@exm-square-not-uniform), and only writing out the arithmetic shows where it fails. Expand every “arith” line before calling the proof done. Which steps may be silent Level 3 leaves things out. If it left out whatever a writer happened to find obvious, a reader could never tell an omission from a gap. So the book fixes the omissions once, by rule. The principle behind the list: a step may go unsaid when a reader who knows the rules can restore it uniquely from what is written. A step that creates something (a name, a witness, a case split), assumes something, or cites something cannot be restored that way, so it is always written. Silent steps are the ones whose result is forced by the lines around them; every choice, assumption and citation is spoken. [Silent Steps] In level-3 prose the following steps may be silent, that is, left unwritten: - ulename{\wedge I} when both conjuncts are displayed, and ulename{\wedge E}; - ulename{R}; - ulename{\vee I}; - ulename{ ot E}, once the contradiction or the empty case has been stated; - the name of ulename{DS} (not the fact it uses); - the exchange of the two members of a disjunction or of a conjunction (@exm-disjunction-first-derivations, @prp-and-commutes), made only to match the schema of a rule, with no citation; - ulename{{\leftrightarrow}E} with a definition (“that is”, “by definition”, or nothing); - ulename{ orall E} at the obvious term; - ulename{{	o}E} whose implication is a line already in the proof that is neither a hypothesis of the statement, nor a cited result, nor an instance of one (for instance, an instance of the property of a chosen name), when its antecedent is displayed or has just been assumed; - ulename{{=}I} and ulename{{=}E} inside a c