Checking Proofs, and What Is Not Allowed A reader working alone must judge proofs, including their own, and is the person least able to do it. The remedy is procedural. This section gives two questions to ask of every line and a catalogue of the errors that recur, each tied to the rule of Chapters 1–4 that it breaks, and then checks four short proofs line by line. Two questions for every line A proof is correct when every line is licensed: by @def-derivation, each line is a premise (a hypothesis, a proved result or a true school fact), an assumption, or one rule applied to available lines. So checking a proof means asking two questions of every line, with the answers in two columns. - Is it true? Is the line correct whenever the hypotheses and the assumptions of the open boxes are true? - Does it follow, and by which rule? From which available lines, and by which rule? The first column finds where to look; the second decides. The two questions are different. A line can be true and not follow: a correct fact the proof has not earned. This is the dangerous case, because nothing about the line looks wrong. A line can also follow and be false: false in a situation where the hypotheses and open assumptions are true. Since the rules never lead from true lines to a false one (@thm-soundness; Section 4.11 for predicate logic), some line it rests on, neither a hypothesis nor an open assumption, is false there too; follow it back until the second column fails. (Proof C’s “1 is even” is true given its box’s assumption.) A proof that n^2 \ge n for every integer n opens with the line “n^2 - n = n(n - 1) \ge 0”. Is the line true? Does it follow? Would the answers change if n ranged over the reals? For an integer n the line is true: no integer lies strictly between 0 and 1, so n \le 0 (both factors \le 0) or n \ge 1 (both \ge 0). But as written it follows from nothing: it is the goal rearranged, without the case split that makes it true. For real n it is false at n = 1/2, where n(n - 1) = -1/4. The first column depends on the situation; the second fails in both. The catalogue Most wrong proofs fail in one of a few ways, each breaking a particular rule. [What is not allowed] Each entry names the error, the rule it breaks and where that rule lives, and a failing instance whose conclusion is marked true or false: a wrong argument can reach either. 1. Verifying instances and concluding “for all”. Breaks ulename{ orall I}, which needs one proof about a fresh name (@def-rules-universal, Section 4.4). Instance: n^2 - n + 11 is prime for n = 0, 1, \dots, 10 (the values are 11, 11, 13, 17, 23, 31, 41, 53, 67, 83, 101), “so it is prime for every n \in 
N”. False: at n = 11 the value is 121 = 11^2. 2. A witness that depends on a variable introduced later, while uniformity is claimed. Breaks the side conditions of ulename{\exists E} and ulename{ orall I}: a witness \delta_0 obtained inside the box of x_0 may not leave it (Section 4.10, @exm-converse-fails). Instance: “x^2 is continuous at every x, with \delta = \min\{1, \varepsilon/(2\abs{x} + 1)\}; so one \delta for each \varepsilon serves every x, and x^2 is uniformly continuous on 
R”. False (@exm-square-not-uniform). 3. Treating the name from ulename{\exists E} as arbitrary. Breaks the side condition of ulename{ orall I}: the witness appears in the open assumption of its box (@def-rules-existential, Section 4.6). Instance: “Some element of A is positive; let x be such an element. Since x was arbitrary, every element of A is positive.” False for A = \{-1, 1\}. 4. Reusing one witness name for two ulename{\exists E}’s. Breaks the fresh-name condition: the second name may not appear in an open assumption, and the first box’s assumption mentions it (Section 4.6, @exm-sum-of-evens). Sibling boxes may share a name. Instance: “m and n are even, so m = 2k and n = 2k for an integer k; hence m = n”. False: m = 2, n = 4. 5. Proving the converse. Breaks ulename{{	o}I}, whose box opens with the hypothesis, not the conclusion (@def-rules-implication, Section 1.5). Instance: “If x^2 > 1, then x > 1: suppose x > 1; then x^2 > x > 1.” False: x = -2. Argument B of Section 1.1 makes the same move with a true conclusion. 6. Affirming the consequent; denying the antecedent. Breaks @def-derivation (Section 1.5), being no rule: P 	o Q with Q gives no P, and with 
